  "summary": "DOAC dose review, week of 10/19/2026 (list grp-doac-review, 9 patients), done under RBC-PHARM-07. I changed 8 doses and left 1 as it was. Each patient has a progress note with the values used, the rule result and the records it relies on.\n\nApixaban (§2):\n- pt-0040 Dorothy Ashworth: 5 → 2.5 mg twice daily. Age 83, plus creatinine 150 µmol/L from the outside lab on 10/15, which is 1.70 mg/dL. Two criteria met.\n- pt-0001 Walter Kowalczyk: 5 → 2.5 mg twice daily. Age 84, measured weight 57.6 kg. Two criteria met.\n- pt-0041 Raymond Duquette: 5 → 2.5 mg twice daily. No criteria met, but he has an active itraconazole order (mr-00359). Itraconazole was left alone.\n- pt-0045 Gregory Lindahl: 5 → 2.5 mg twice daily. No criteria met, but the St. Brigid infectious disease letter of 9/18 lists darunavir boosted with ritonavir. His HIV medicines were not changed.\n- pt-0002 Harold Vance: 2.5 → 5 mg twice daily. The hospital lowered his dose in February. Now only his age (82) meets a criterion: weight is 64.9 kg (143 lb) and creatinine is 1.21 mg/dL.\n\nRivaroxaban (§3, creatinine clearance by Cockcroft-Gault with actual weight):\n- pt-0039 Clarence Bauer: 20 → 15 mg daily. Clearance 44 mL/min, using the 59 kg weight measured 10/14. I did not use the 168 lb weight he reported by phone on 10/21, because it wasn't measured.\n- pt-0003 Frank Delgado: 20 → 15 mg daily. Clearance 44 mL/min, using the outside creatinine of 160 µmol/L (1.81 mg/dL) from 10/19.\n- pt-0038 Eugene Moravec: 15 → 20 mg daily. Clearance 56 mL/min with his actual weight of 96 kg.\n- pt-0043 Marjorie Pellegrino: no change. Clearance 59 mL/min, so her current 20 mg daily is correct. I wrote a note saying so.\n\nNone of the patients on 2.5 mg twice daily is also taking a listed inhibitor, so there are no combination warnings to send you. Nothing else on the list needs your action.\n\nThe new rivaroxaban orders are set to once daily, but the order tool had no field for \"with the evening meal\". That instruction is in each order's reason and each patient's note, but it may not be on the new prescription itself.",
